    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i am having a bit of an issue with some walnut joinery. i am cutting dovetails for a cabinet set and the fibers are splintering right at the tips of the pins every time i pare them down. it's making the joints look messy and i'm worried about the strength. any idea what i might be doing wrong with my chisels or the grain direction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's possible that you're running away from the choke, a common issue with diamond-coated chisels when cutting through hardwood. Chisels are supposed to apply pressure through the base of the cutter, not at the tip. Try engaging the choke by applying gentle, downward pressure along the length of the blade, rather than focusing on the tip. Also, pay attention to the grain direction, making sure you're always cutting from the end grain of the wood to reduce splintering.",
      "source": "target",
