    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah that makes sense. i'll grab the hygrometer and check it out. i've also been thinking about the bevel angle on my paring chisels. i usually go with a 25 degree angle for most things, but maybe for these fine pins i should be going even steeper? or is there something else about the way the edge is ground that could be causing the fibers to tear instead of slicing clean?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For fine cuts like dovetail pins, a steeper bevel angle can actually be beneficial, especially if you're dealing with a hardwood like walnut. Try applying a bevel angle of 30 or 36 degrees to see if that helps reduce splintering, as it will give you a more aggressive, slicing cut. However, be careful not to go too steep, as this can lead to vibration and loss of control. Also, consider checking the ribbing (the beveled edge) of your chisel to ensure it's suitably honed for a clean, slicing action.",
